/**
 * Karnataka, as geometry.
 *
 * District and taluk boundaries from the geohacker/india dataset, simplified
 * with Douglas-Peucker to a few hundred points each — enough to be
 * recognisably Karnataka at the size drawn, small enough to ship in the
 * bundle and render with no tile server, no API key and no network call. A
 * map that stalls because a tile provider is slow is a map that fails in a
 * meeting.
 *
 * Centroids are computed from the real polygons rather than typed, which is
 * what lets a pin be placed honestly at all.
 *
 * A pin marks a TALUK, because that is the finest unit the register actually
 * holds for these sites. Every parcel carries a district and a taluk; none
 * carries a village or a coordinate, and the polygon on a parcel record is a
 * schematic of its shape rather than its position. Placing seventy-eight dots
 * would mean inventing seventy-eight positions — more precise-looking and
 * less true. When sites are registered with a village, as the field app
 * captures them, village centroids become the right unit and this file is
 * where they would go.
 *
 * The boundary files predate the 2014 renamings and use several older
 * spellings, so Belgaum, Bellary, Bijapur, Gulbarga, Mysore and Tumkur are
 * mapped to their current district names, and Bail Hongal, Saundatti, Sindgi,
 * Chitapur, Deodrug and Hosdurga to the taluk names the register uses.
 */

export type Shape = { name: string; d: string; cx: number; cy: number };

